 \n<div class="dp-content-block dp-margin-direction-tblr dp-padding-directi
 on-tblr dp-border-style-solid dp-border-dir-all" style="padding: 0px 15px 
 10px\; background-color: #fffbf5\; color: #000000\; margin-top: 25px\; bor
 der-color: #b38f6c\; border-width: 1px\; border-radius: 0px\; margin-left:
  20px\; margin-right: 20px\;">\n<p class="lead">For most archaeological pr
 ojects\, samples are collected for various purposes\, but the most common 
 type of sample collected for determining the age or date of a site or mate
 rials is radiocarbon sampling. You read in Archaeology Essentials (pp.120-
 127) how radioactive carbon is absorbed by organic (living) organisms\, an
 d after they die\, the radioactive carbon begins to decay. When collecting
  samples for radiocarbon dating\, it is important to collect the correct t
 ype of material and not to contaminate the sample during collection. Read 
 the following instruction and complete the assignment.</p>\n</div>\n<div c
 lass="dp-content-block dp-margin-direction-tblr dp-padding-direction-tblr 
 dp-border-style-solid dp-border-dir-all" style="padding: 0px 15px 10px\; b
 ackground-color: #fffbf5\; color: #000000\; margin-top: 25px\; border-colo
 r: #b38f6c\; border-width: 1px\; border-radius: 0px\; margin-left: 20px\; 
 margin-right: 20px\;">\n<h3 class="dp-margin-direction-tblr dp-has-icon" s
 tyle="background-color: #f1e6d4\; color: #000000\; padding-top: 10px\; pad
 ding-left: 15px\; margin: 0px -15px 20px\; padding-bottom: 10px\; text-ali
 gn: left\; border-left: 5px #3c0a00\; border-top-style: none\; border-bott
 om: 2px solid #3c0a00\; font-size: 22px\; border-top-color: #3c0a00\; bord
 er-right-color: #3c0a00\;"><i class="dp-icon fas fa-feather-alt"><span cla
 ss="dp-icon-content" style="display: none\;">&nbsp\;</span></i>&nbsp\; Int
 roduction</h3>\n<p>Below is a scenario that I encountered when working in 
 western Provo at a Fremont archaeological site. Closely review some of the
  ways samples can be contaminated on p. 123 of the book (under heading “
 Contamination and Interpretation of Radiocarbon Samples). Then\, create a 
 scenario of how the radiocarbon samples that were collected could have bee
 n contaminated by Dr. Searcy or members of his crew.&nbsp\;</p>\n<p><stron
 g>Setting</strong>:</p>\n<p>The Hinckley Mounds site is located at an acti
 ve farm and ranch in west Provo. The Hinckley family have been farming the
  land since 1898. Cattle\, horses\, and other animals have also grazed the
  land where the site is being excavated. In addition to the farm animals\,
  the Hinckley family has used this plot of land to store hay underneath a 
 covered structured (hay barrack)\, and to store farm equipment and supplie
 s. They also used part of the land as a burn pile where old trees\, brush
 \, and other items would be piled over time and occasionally burned. The a
 rchaeological crew spends their day at the site\, and this includes eating
  snacks and meals during the day. There are also several visitors to the s
 ite every day who walk around the excavation areas.&nbsp\;</p>\n<p><strong
 >Sample collection</strong>:</p>\n<p>In 2023\, Dr. Searcy’s crew identif
 ied a Fremont pithouse about 70 cm under the surface of the modern ground 
 surface. They found many different artifacts\, including many organic rema
 ins that were collected for radiocarbon sampling\, but the several of the 
 samples resulted in dates from the modern era (1900s-2000s). This suggests
  they were contaminated.&nbsp\;</p>\n</div>\n<div class="dp-content-block 
 dp-margin-direction-tblr dp-padding-direction-tblr dp-border-style-solid d
 p-border-dir-all" style="padding: 0px 15px 10px\; background-color: #fffbf
 5\; color: #000000\; margin-top: 25px\; border-color: #b38f6c\; border-wid
 th: 1px\; border-radius: 0px\; margin-left: 20px\; margin-right: 20px\;">
 \n<h3 class="dp-margin-direction-tblr dp-has-icon" style="background-color
 : #f1e6d4\; color: #000000\; padding-top: 10px\; padding-left: 15px\; marg
 in: 0px -15px 20px\; padding-bottom: 10px\; text-align: left\; border-left
 : 5px #3c0a00\; border-top-style: none\; border-bottom: 2px solid #3c0a00
 \; font-size: 22px\; border-top-color: #3c0a00\; border-right-color: #3c0a
 00\; border-radius: 0px\;"><i class="fas fa-tasks"><span class="dp-icon-co
 ntent" style="display: none\;">&nbsp\;</span></i>&nbsp\; Instructions</h3>
 \n<p>By <strong>Wednesday\, Oct 14th </strong>(Part 1):</p>\n<p>For this d
 iscussion\, you will write a 2-3 sentence scenario that:&nbsp\;</p>\n<ol>
 \n<li>Includes the type of organic sample collected. Think about the types
  of organic things that the indigenous Fremont might have had in their hou
 ses living near Utah Lake 1000 years ago. (5 pts)</li>\n<li>And how the sa
 mple might have been contaminated during collection (5 pts).&nbsp\;</li>\n
 </ol>\n<p>By<strong> Friday\, Oct 16th </strong>(Part 2):</p>\n<p>You shou
 ld respond to one of your classmate’s scenarios with a solution about ho
 w to prevent contamination in the future. Your 2-3 sentence response shoul
 d include the following (5 pts):&nbsp\;</p>\n<ol style="list-style-type: d
 ecimal\;">\n<li>State specifically what may have or did contaminate the ra
 diocarbon sample\, and&nbsp\;</li>\n<li>How could you prevent this contami
 nation from happening in the future?&nbsp\;</li>\n</ol>\n<div class="dp-ca
 llout dp-callout-placeholder card dp-callout-position-default dp-callout-t
 ype-default dp-callout-color-dp-primary" style="border-color: #000000 #000
 000 #000000 #5f7c6c\;">\n<div class="card-body">\n<p>Remember that while t
 he excavation was real\, I want you to be creative in your scenario\, whic
 h can be completely fictitious.</p>\n<p>For example\, you could say\,</p>
 \n<ul>\n<li>“While students were collecting the radiocarbon sample\, Dr.
  Searcy fell into the excavation area\, which caused organic materials fro
 m a different stratigraphic layer to fall into the sample bag.”</li>\n</
 ul>\n<p>Your response with a solution to a fellow student’s scenario can
  be just as creative\, such as\,</p>\n<ul>\n<li>“Make sure to keep peopl
 e\, especially Dr. Searcy\, from walking on or around the excavation area 
 during sampling.”</li>\n</ul>\n<p class="cp-txt-dp-accent">(By the way\,
  you can’t reuse this example for this assignment.)&nbsp\;</p>\n</div>\n
 </div>\n</div>\n<div class="dp-panels-wrapper dp-accordion-default dp-padd
 ing-direction-tblr dp-margin-direction-tblr" style="margin-left: 20px\; ma
 rgin-right: 20px\; color: #fffbf5\;">\n<div class="dp-panel-group">\n<h3 c
 lass="dp-panel-heading" style="background-color: #d8c3a5\; color: #000000
 \; border-color: #d8c3a5\;">Image Credits</h3>\n<div class="dp-panel-conte
 nt" style="background-color: #fffbf5\; color: #000000\;">\n<p>ChatGPT\, re
 sponse to \\"5:1 banne image that is petroglyph-style\,\\" OpenAI\, June 3
 \, 2026\, https://chat.openai.com.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div class
 ="dp-progress-placeholder dp-module-progress-completion dp-padding-directi
 on-tblr dp-margin-direction-tblr dp-border-style-solid dp-border-dir-all" 
 style="display: none\; margin: 20px\; background-color: #8a4b2a\; color: #
 ffffff\; border-radius: 0px\; border-width: 1px\; border-color: #5f7c6c\;"
 >Module Item Completion (browser only)</div>\n<p>&nbsp\;</p>\n</div><scrip
