DESCRIPTION:*************************************************\nLesson 7 Di
 scuss: Collecting Radiocarbon Samples\n\n*********************************
 ****************\n\n[] (https://byu.instructure.com/courses/34744/files/13
 112831/download?download_frd=1)\n\n \n\nFor most archaeological projects
 \, samples are collected for various purposes\, but the most common type o
 f sample collected for determining the age or date of a site or materials 
 is radiocarbon sampling. You read in Archaeology Essentials (pp.120-127) h
 ow radioactive carbon is absorbed by organic (living) organisms\, and afte
 r they die\, the radioactive carbon begins to decay. When collecting sampl
 es for radiocarbon dating\, it is important to collect the correct type of
  material and not to contaminate the sample during collection. Read the fo
 llowing instruction and complete the assignment.\n\n   Introduction\n---
 ------------\n\nBelow is a scenario that I encountered when working in wes
 tern Provo at a Fremont archaeological site. Closely review some of the wa
 ys samples can be contaminated on p. 123 of the book (under heading “Con
 tamination and Interpretation of Radiocarbon Samples). Then\, create a sce
 nario of how the radiocarbon samples that were collected could have been c
 ontaminated by Dr. Searcy or members of his crew. \n\nSetting:\n\nThe Hin
 ckley Mounds site is located at an active farm and ranch in west Provo. Th
 e Hinckley family have been farming the land since 1898. Cattle\, horses\,
  and other animals have also grazed the land where the site is being excav
 ated. In addition to the farm animals\, the Hinckley family has used this 
 plot of land to store hay underneath a covered structured (hay barrack)\, 
 and to store farm equipment and supplies. They also used part of the land 
 as a burn pile where old trees\, brush\, and other items would be piled ov
 er time and occasionally burned. The archaeological crew spends their day 
 at the site\, and this includes eating snacks and meals during the day. Th
 ere are also several visitors to the site every day who walk around the ex
 cavation areas. \n\nSample collection:\n\nIn 2023\, Dr. Searcy’s crew i
 dentified a Fremont pithouse about 70 cm under the surface of the modern g
 round surface. They found many different artifacts\, including many organi
 c remains that were collected for radiocarbon sampling\, but the several o
 f the samples resulted in dates from the modern era (1900s-2000s). This su
 ggests they were contaminated. \n\n   Instructions\n---------------\n\n
 By Wednesday\, Oct 14th (Part 1):\n\nFor this discussion\, you will write 
 a 2-3 sentence scenario that: \n\n* Includes the type of organic sample c
 ollected. Think about the types of organic things that the indigenous Frem
 ont might have had in their houses living near Utah Lake 1000 years ago. (
 5 pts)\n\n* And how the sample might have been contaminated during collect
 ion (5 pts). \n\nBy Friday\, Oct 16th (Part 2):\n\nYou should respond to 
 one of your classmate’s scenarios with a solution about how to prevent c
 ontamination in the future. Your 2-3 sentence response should include the 
 following (5 pts): \n\n* State specifically what may have or did contamin
 ate the radiocarbon sample\, and \n\n* How could you prevent this contami
 nation from happening in the future? \n\nRemember that while the excavati
 on was real\, I want you to be creative in your scenario\, which can be co
 mpletely fictitious.\n\nFor example\, you could say\,\n\n* “While studen
 ts were collecting the radiocarbon sample\, Dr. Searcy fell into the excav
 ation area\, which caused organic materials from a different stratigraphic
  layer to fall into the sample bag.”\n\nYour response with a solution to
  a fellow student’s scenario can be just as creative\, such as\,\n\n* 
 “Make sure to keep people\, especially Dr. Searcy\, from walking on or a
 round the excavation area during sampling.”\n\n(By the way\, you can’t
  reuse this example for this assignment.) \n\nImage Credits\n------------
 -\n\nChatGPT\, response to \\"5:1 banne image that is petroglyph-style\,\
 \" OpenAI\, June 3\, 2026\, https://chat.openai.com.\n\nModule Item Comple
 tion (browser only)\n\n 
